WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.411 --> 00:00:04.078
(water lapping)

00:00:16.233 --> 00:00:18.983
(tranquil music)

00:00:20.739 --> 00:00:22.339
- When we first started measuring ice

00:00:22.339 --> 00:00:25.475
back in the early 1990s,
nobody knew basically

00:00:25.475 --> 00:00:26.904
what Greenland was doing.

00:00:26.904 --> 00:00:28.227
Was it getting bigger or getting smaller?

00:00:28.227 --> 00:00:30.870
That was a total unknown.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.260
And so, in 2000, we
published our first results,

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.416
using an older version of the technology

00:00:35.416 --> 00:00:38.361
that we're using now in Ice Bridge.

00:00:38.361 --> 00:00:40.493
And what that showed us
was that the ice sheet

00:00:40.493 --> 00:00:42.264
was not in balance.

00:00:42.264 --> 00:00:45.037
It was delivering ice into the
oceans and raising sea level,

00:00:45.037 --> 00:00:49.792
even at that time, and that
process has accelerated.

00:00:49.792 --> 00:00:52.760
It's retreated kilometers since that time.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.081
At, I think it's fair to
say: an alarming rate.

00:00:55.081 --> 00:00:57.697
- Ice sheets are like bathtubs
full of water, essentially.

00:00:57.697 --> 00:00:59.443
And so if you take the plug out,

00:00:59.443 --> 00:01:03.355
then, it's gonna go into the
ocean and sea levels will rise.

00:01:03.355 --> 00:01:06.105
(tranquil music)

00:01:08.475 --> 00:01:11.054
- The majority of the
people of North America

00:01:11.054 --> 00:01:12.887
live on the shoreline.

00:01:14.022 --> 00:01:16.985
You go to European continent, the same.

00:01:16.985 --> 00:01:19.014
South America, the same.

00:01:19.014 --> 00:01:22.065
That means majority of where people live

00:01:22.065 --> 00:01:25.158
will be afflicted by the change.

00:01:25.158 --> 00:01:27.694
If we are true believers in spirit,

00:01:27.694 --> 00:01:30.361
we cannot just watch them drown.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:49.400
(spacey musical theme)

00:02:15.782 --> 00:02:18.961
- Whoa! (laughing)

00:02:18.961 --> 00:02:19.794
I give up.

00:02:21.176 --> 00:02:22.176
I surrender.

00:02:23.799 --> 00:02:27.579
We are at the terminus of Russel Glacier,

00:02:27.579 --> 00:02:29.499
of the Greenland ice sheet.

00:02:29.499 --> 00:02:31.782
And so what we're standing
in front of, right here,

00:02:31.782 --> 00:02:35.086
is where, essentially, the
glacier comes to an end.

00:02:35.086 --> 00:02:38.929
So, you can see it's melting,
you can hear it, actually.

00:02:38.929 --> 00:02:42.363
You can see the icicles hanging
from the front of the cliff,

00:02:42.363 --> 00:02:44.347
which indicates that the melt has started,

00:02:44.347 --> 00:02:46.523
and you can hear the ice
kind of cracking and popping,

00:02:46.523 --> 00:02:51.306
which means that it's actively
breaking off and falling.

00:02:51.306 --> 00:02:53.926
It's a dynamic landscape, as you can see.

00:02:53.926 --> 00:02:55.953
Things are falling, you
can hear liquid water.

00:02:55.953 --> 00:02:58.469
I mean, this is just a
changing, evolving landscape,

00:02:58.469 --> 00:03:01.713
and you can visibly see that.

00:03:01.713 --> 00:03:05.083
It's like, if, I don't wanna leave. (laughing)

00:03:05.083 --> 00:03:06.403
I wanna stay here.

00:03:06.403 --> 00:03:08.320
Um, it's just beyond words.

00:03:14.737 --> 00:03:17.827
I went to a very small liberal
arts school in Vermont,

00:03:17.827 --> 00:03:21.966
and I think I applied to like
15 different grad schools,

00:03:21.966 --> 00:03:24.334
because I had no idea what I wanted to do.

00:03:24.334 --> 00:03:28.472
And I ended up going from
Vermont all the way to Oregon.

00:03:28.472 --> 00:03:32.639
And I'd never seen a glacier
before, until I went to Oregon.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.118
And I just was, I fell in love.

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:41.205
I absolutely fell in love,
and I don't even know

00:03:41.205 --> 00:03:44.769
if I can necessarily say
what it was about them,

00:03:44.769 --> 00:03:48.840
but the landscape was so
different and dramatic.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.933
To me, it's a very moving landscape,

00:03:50.933 --> 00:03:53.710
and it truly touched me
as it pretty much directed

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:56.627
my career choice in life, so, yeah.

00:03:57.656 --> 00:04:01.823
It was love at first
sight, I guess. (laughing)

00:04:09.219 --> 00:04:11.552
(muttering)

00:04:14.509 --> 00:04:16.366
- A couple of days ago, she was
like, shoving it in my face.

00:04:16.366 --> 00:04:17.368
Like: open this!

00:04:17.368 --> 00:04:19.267
- That's totally exaggerated, by the way.

00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:20.739
If you're coming to a place like this,

00:04:20.739 --> 00:04:23.384
you have to be a person that
gets along with other people

00:04:23.384 --> 00:04:25.944
and is super friendly and outgoing.

00:04:25.944 --> 00:04:28.352
Because if you come here
and you're not like that,

00:04:28.352 --> 00:04:29.975
then you're probably gonna be miserable

00:04:29.975 --> 00:04:32.142
and never come back again.

00:04:36.291 --> 00:04:37.124
- Woo!

00:04:38.595 --> 00:04:41.678
(acoustic strumming)

00:04:43.373 --> 00:04:45.720
- I've eaten more meals
with some of the folks here

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.471
that I'm in the field
with than I have with the

00:04:48.471 --> 00:04:51.822
other most important people
in my life, back home.

00:04:51.822 --> 00:04:53.635
(laughing)

00:04:53.635 --> 00:04:56.238
We spend 16 hours a day
together, most days.

00:04:56.238 --> 00:04:57.961
You can't spend that kind
of time with each other

00:04:57.961 --> 00:05:01.251
and not basically become a family.

00:05:01.251 --> 00:05:02.616
We're a long ways from our families.

00:05:02.616 --> 00:05:04.387
We're a long ways from
our logistical trains.

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:06.584
We're a long ways from most things,

00:05:06.584 --> 00:05:08.120
and so we have to depend on each other,

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:09.994
and ultimately, for me,
that becomes probably

00:05:09.994 --> 00:05:11.470
the most rewarding part of this whole job,

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:14.113
is getting to work with such a great team.

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:17.780
(overlapping conversations)

00:05:24.803 --> 00:05:27.386
(dogs barking)

00:05:32.803 --> 00:05:34.787
(muttering)

00:05:34.787 --> 00:05:36.468
(laughing)

00:05:36.468 --> 00:05:38.135
- [Man's Voice] So good.

00:05:38.135 --> 00:05:39.241
So good.

00:05:39.241 --> 00:05:40.074
I love it.

00:05:43.086 --> 00:05:44.753
- Well, here we are.

00:05:47.137 --> 00:05:48.887
Welcome to Greenland.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.777
(sweeping ambient music)

00:06:14.019 --> 00:06:17.102
- [Brooke] Some snacks for the plane.

00:06:21.251 --> 00:06:24.001
(sonorous music)

00:07:23.459 --> 00:07:26.292
(engines whining)

00:07:36.944 --> 00:07:38.127
- [Man On Radio] Okay, sounds good.

00:07:38.127 --> 00:07:39.642
So, that's coherent noise, right?

00:07:39.642 --> 00:07:41.975
(muttering)

00:07:44.356 --> 00:07:47.106
(tranquil music)

00:07:51.332 --> 00:07:54.415
(muttering on radio)

00:07:56.516 --> 00:07:59.140
- Having the chance to
actually see the ice,

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:00.973
it connects you to it.

00:08:05.882 --> 00:08:07.482
It's sort of an inspirational thing,

00:08:07.482 --> 00:08:09.055
from a scientific standpoint,

00:08:09.055 --> 00:08:12.708
to be able to look at
something that you've been,

00:08:12.708 --> 00:08:14.479
you know, building your career off of

00:08:14.479 --> 00:08:16.591
and looking at how beautiful it is.

00:08:16.591 --> 00:08:19.258
(uptempo music)

00:08:22.522 --> 00:08:25.103
And you lose that
inspiration when you're just

00:08:25.103 --> 00:08:27.603
working at a computer all day.

00:08:31.626 --> 00:08:34.165
- [Man On Radio] Right turn
25 miles, copy, thanks.

00:08:34.165 --> 00:08:36.004
- [Brooke] Hey Aaron, any
plan to go back to the dump

00:08:36.004 --> 00:08:38.244
to pick up that bench?

00:08:38.244 --> 00:08:40.932
- I could use a new bed. (laughing)

00:08:40.932 --> 00:08:44.666
You can't blame me if it breaks, John.

00:08:44.666 --> 00:08:46.755
- Operation IceBridge is a NASA program

00:08:46.755 --> 00:08:50.222
meant to fill in knowledge
about the Earth's cryosphere.

00:08:50.222 --> 00:08:52.804
The Earth's ice cover.

00:08:52.804 --> 00:08:54.234
The idea is we're trying to measure

00:08:54.234 --> 00:08:56.666
everything about the ice,
from the top of the ice,

00:08:56.666 --> 00:08:59.439
all the way down to where
it meets the bedrock.

00:08:59.439 --> 00:09:00.909
And why would we wanna do that?

00:09:00.909 --> 00:09:03.748
Well, the top of the ice
is about taking a snapshot

00:09:03.748 --> 00:09:06.968
of what the state of the
Earth's ice cover is now.

00:09:06.968 --> 00:09:10.746
And how that compares to
how it was in prior years.

00:09:10.746 --> 00:09:12.492
But in order to understand those changes,

00:09:12.492 --> 00:09:15.652
we have to put them in context.

00:09:15.652 --> 00:09:16.996
- Most people would be very disappointed

00:09:16.996 --> 00:09:18.742
if they were plopped in
the middle of an ice sheet,

00:09:18.742 --> 00:09:20.858
well, for many reasons, they
would be disappointed, but if

00:09:20.858 --> 00:09:23.437
you're plopped in the middle
of an ice sheet, it's flat

00:09:23.437 --> 00:09:27.023
and white and boring, and
it's just covered in snow.

00:09:27.023 --> 00:09:29.860
But the interesting part
is when you have a radar

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:32.954
that you can actually
see internal horizons,

00:09:32.954 --> 00:09:36.602
underneath the surface, and
you can use those internal

00:09:36.602 --> 00:09:39.844
horizons to actually
look back through time.

00:09:39.844 --> 00:09:42.511
So it's sort of like tree rings.

00:09:43.514 --> 00:09:45.903
Basically, the ice sheet
is its own time capsule.

00:09:45.903 --> 00:09:50.468
You can go back in time and
understand how it's changing.

00:09:50.468 --> 00:09:53.604
If you get more snowfall
than ice mass lost,

00:09:53.604 --> 00:09:55.994
then the ice sheet is growing,

00:09:55.994 --> 00:09:57.804
but if you get less
snowfall than ice mass loss,

00:09:57.804 --> 00:09:59.471
then it's shrinking.

00:09:59.471 --> 00:10:02.521
And so it's a key
ingredient in understanding

00:10:02.521 --> 00:10:06.271
the glacier's contribution
to sea level rise.

00:10:08.964 --> 00:10:12.047
(murmuring on radio)

00:10:30.895 --> 00:10:33.242
- Generally, I'm like
a little kid out here.

00:10:33.242 --> 00:10:34.869
I get to play around with computers,

00:10:34.869 --> 00:10:37.060
and lasers, and airplanes and weather.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:39.919
And we fly this airplane at
1,500 feet above the surface.

00:10:39.919 --> 00:10:43.098
So there are many, many,
many times where we are down

00:10:43.098 --> 00:10:45.115
in a glacier valley, collecting
detailed measurements

00:10:45.115 --> 00:10:47.044
of that glacier, and the
walls of the canyon are

00:10:47.044 --> 00:10:48.835
way above us on both
sides of the airplane.

00:10:48.835 --> 00:10:50.927
It is spectacular to be flying that way.

00:10:50.927 --> 00:10:52.804
Every day, that's the view
from our office window,

00:10:52.804 --> 00:10:55.599
that hardly anybody ever gets to see.

00:10:55.599 --> 00:10:57.604
And anybody, anywhere in the world,

00:10:57.604 --> 00:11:00.058
regardless of their nationality,
can access our data,

00:11:00.058 --> 00:11:02.170
as long as they have
an internet connection.

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:04.303
My hope is that that
open data policy leads

00:11:04.303 --> 00:11:05.711
to a better world.

00:11:05.711 --> 00:11:07.844
But knowledge of the Earth's
cryosphere is, frankly,

00:11:07.844 --> 00:11:09.466
in its infancy right now, in my opinion.

00:11:09.466 --> 00:11:11.514
And we have a long way to
go to really understand

00:11:11.514 --> 00:11:13.583
what's going on up here.

00:11:13.583 --> 00:11:16.314
- [Man On Radio] Okay, let's
debrief starting with ATM.

00:11:16.314 --> 00:11:18.575
- [Man On Radio] Okay,
had a great day today.

00:11:18.575 --> 00:11:20.856
Weather cooperated.

00:11:20.856 --> 00:11:23.139
Our data volume's T-5.

00:11:23.139 --> 00:11:25.188
Collected 31 gigabytes.

00:11:25.188 --> 00:11:27.642
T-6, collected 162 gigabytes.

00:11:27.642 --> 00:11:28.559
Cambot, 52.

00:11:29.668 --> 00:11:32.585
(melancholy music)

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:50.105
- I'm lucky today.

00:11:50.105 --> 00:11:52.858
I don't know how people
would study ice in the '50s.

00:11:52.858 --> 00:11:55.525
What did you, go to the ice and,

00:11:56.713 --> 00:11:59.002
you know, make a measurement, dig a hole,

00:11:59.002 --> 00:12:00.346
and then come back?

00:12:00.346 --> 00:12:02.179
I mean, how are you supposed
to make an inference

00:12:02.179 --> 00:12:06.170
about the entire ice sheet,
just from that one data point?

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:08.729
Mainly, people thought that
these ice sheets responded

00:12:08.729 --> 00:12:11.695
on such long timescales
that they would never

00:12:11.695 --> 00:12:14.746
be able to change anything,
to actually impact

00:12:14.746 --> 00:12:16.730
the ice sheets enough that would cause

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:19.980
a visible change over human timescales.

00:12:21.743 --> 00:12:24.939
With the advent of
satellites, they opened a lot

00:12:24.939 --> 00:12:29.679
of people's eyes to, whoa,
these actually are changing

00:12:29.679 --> 00:12:32.762
on timescales that we can understand.

00:12:33.775 --> 00:12:35.588
And so that was a big difference.

00:12:35.588 --> 00:12:38.447
Because for a while it was:
oh, I study an ice sheet.

00:12:38.447 --> 00:12:40.645
And it changes over thousands of years.

00:12:40.645 --> 00:12:42.799
But now it's like,
whoa, these can actually

00:12:42.799 --> 00:12:45.636
have dramatic changes right now,

00:12:45.636 --> 00:12:49.803
and we can see them and it can
impact, basically, everyone.

00:12:53.999 --> 00:12:56.249
(rumbling)

00:12:58.436 --> 00:13:00.496
- [Female Reporter] Devastation
of Katrina, catastrophic.

00:13:00.496 --> 00:13:02.468
- [Male Reporter] 30 people dead

00:13:02.468 --> 00:13:04.239
in one apartment complex alone.

00:13:04.239 --> 00:13:07.322
(overlapping shouts)

00:13:08.655 --> 00:13:10.869
- [Male Reporter] Hundreds
of houses trashed,

00:13:10.869 --> 00:13:12.922
as the ocean crashed on through.

00:13:12.922 --> 00:13:14.884
- [Male Reporter] Hurricane
Harvey slammed ashore

00:13:14.884 --> 00:13:16.355
Friday night as a category four.

00:13:16.355 --> 00:13:18.762
- Do not come back to
the evacuation zones.

00:13:18.762 --> 00:13:22.180
It has just begun, and
it's going to get worse.

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:25.493
- It's hard to get people
excited about ice sheets

00:13:25.493 --> 00:13:27.661
when you tell them, oh,
they're contributing

00:13:27.661 --> 00:13:29.964
a millimeter per year to sea level,

00:13:29.964 --> 00:13:33.131
and it's a hard number to tell people.

00:13:34.234 --> 00:13:36.943
So, you know, you think: this much?

00:13:36.943 --> 00:13:40.719
You know, how is that going to matter?

00:13:40.719 --> 00:13:45.451
But it's the impact of
storms that gets magnified

00:13:45.451 --> 00:13:49.590
once you have an additional
millimeter, that can then impact

00:13:49.590 --> 00:13:53.114
a much larger area if
you have a massive storm.

00:13:53.114 --> 00:13:55.281
We're pushing the extreme.

00:13:56.887 --> 00:13:59.684
So we're getting more extreme storms,

00:13:59.684 --> 00:14:02.308
and then if we have rising
seas to go along with it,

00:14:02.308 --> 00:14:06.475
that means the impact is just
gonna be more widespread.

00:14:14.106 --> 00:14:17.690
Rising sea level is going
to displace a lot of people,

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:20.591
and typically it's a lot of
the people who, financially,

00:14:20.591 --> 00:14:25.050
don't have the means to
necessarily to move from where

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:27.482
they've grown up their entire lives.

00:14:27.482 --> 00:14:30.065
(morose music)

00:14:54.724 --> 00:14:57.307
(wind howling)

00:15:10.788 --> 00:15:13.538
(sonorous music)

00:15:43.855 --> 00:15:46.072
- In the month of June in 1975,

00:15:46.072 --> 00:15:47.736
my mother and father,

00:15:47.736 --> 00:15:51.471
instructed me that I should tell the world

00:15:51.471 --> 00:15:53.888
that this big ice is melting.

00:15:55.158 --> 00:15:57.655
We knew that it was melting
because here on this,

00:15:57.655 --> 00:16:01.822
the water was coming out in
1963, in the month of January.

00:16:03.182 --> 00:16:05.231
Now, you can see it's nothing.

00:16:05.231 --> 00:16:07.231
From being 1,500 meters.

00:16:08.154 --> 00:16:11.076
And I spoke about that
at the United Nations,

00:16:11.076 --> 00:16:13.658
in the August of 1978.

00:16:13.658 --> 00:16:15.770
That was the first time it was brought out

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:19.853
by the native people that
the big ice is melting.

00:16:20.896 --> 00:16:24.899
I began to be invited by
organizations and events,

00:16:24.899 --> 00:16:27.247
and ended up traveling
for the United Nations

00:16:27.247 --> 00:16:28.830
all over the world.

00:16:29.935 --> 00:16:33.412
- Do I look like George Clooney,

00:16:33.412 --> 00:16:34.495
or Brad Pitt?

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:40.596
- [Woman's Voice] Definitely George.

00:16:40.596 --> 00:16:42.138
- You have good taste.

00:16:42.138 --> 00:16:44.591
I am friends with many, many people now.

00:16:44.591 --> 00:16:47.172
And really look forward
to going to that city,

00:16:47.172 --> 00:16:49.348
where the next meeting is going to be.

00:16:49.348 --> 00:16:51.204
And we will look forward
to go to a restaurant

00:16:51.204 --> 00:16:54.084
and have a nice time, and at the end,

00:16:54.084 --> 00:16:58.001
I realized that I was
just like everybody else.

00:16:59.354 --> 00:17:03.521
I wasn't changing as I had been
asked by my elders to change

00:17:04.493 --> 00:17:08.660
and now it is too late to
stop the melting of the ice.

00:17:11.215 --> 00:17:15.382
The idea is to change the
people, and I failed in my work.

00:17:18.703 --> 00:17:21.452
So I decided I would quit that job.

00:17:21.452 --> 00:17:24.804
I was going home to Nook,
where my mother lived.

00:17:24.804 --> 00:17:27.471
(pensive music)

00:17:33.248 --> 00:17:34.331
Welcome home.

00:17:36.687 --> 00:17:39.104
(vocalizing)

00:17:41.484 --> 00:17:45.050
My mother was sitting
in my father's chair.

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:47.457
We took our hands together,

00:17:47.457 --> 00:17:49.124
she closed her eyes.

00:17:50.767 --> 00:17:55.095
She said: Son, you're
just gonna have to learn

00:17:55.095 --> 00:17:57.786
to melt the ice in the heart of man.

00:17:57.786 --> 00:18:00.494
Only by melting the ice
in the heart of man,

00:18:00.494 --> 00:18:03.244
man will have a chance to change.

00:18:07.023 --> 00:18:10.106
And I can still hear her saying that.

00:18:11.716 --> 00:18:14.703
I did not succeed,
because I failed to change

00:18:14.703 --> 00:18:17.036
the leadership of the world.

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.847
We still talk about the same old stuff.

00:18:20.847 --> 00:18:23.300
For the last 40 years,
they've been trying to get

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:24.633
to an agreement.

00:18:26.842 --> 00:18:29.380
No matter what we do, we
cannot stop the melting

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:32.282
of the big ice; we cannot.

00:18:32.282 --> 00:18:36.186
But politically, we
refuse to talk about it.

00:18:36.186 --> 00:18:39.151
Because it's just too overwhelming.

00:18:39.151 --> 00:18:41.568
And… we will scare the people.

00:18:42.753 --> 00:18:45.743
I'd rather scare the
people and wake them up

00:18:45.743 --> 00:18:48.493
than let them drown in ignorance.

00:19:01.273 --> 00:19:04.606
(plane engines buzzing)

00:19:07.418 --> 00:19:08.835
- Bye, IceBridge.

00:19:11.343 --> 00:19:15.510
Go forth and science,
IceBridge. (laughing)

00:19:22.268 --> 00:19:24.826
The slice of time that we
live in, our Earth looks

00:19:24.826 --> 00:19:28.278
like this because of how
big the ice sheets are.

00:19:28.278 --> 00:19:29.924
If they weren't there,
our planet would look

00:19:29.924 --> 00:19:32.313
entirely different, because all that water

00:19:32.313 --> 00:19:34.532
would be in the ocean.

00:19:34.532 --> 00:19:36.218
Just keep that in mind.

00:19:36.218 --> 00:19:38.372
That, as these start to change,

00:19:38.372 --> 00:19:42.455
our planet, as a whole,
will start to change, so.

00:19:54.671 --> 00:19:57.058
- There's really only one
place for that ice to go.

00:19:57.058 --> 00:19:59.407
It has to go into the
oceans and raise sea level.

00:19:59.407 --> 00:20:01.451
And that's important not
only for the United States

00:20:01.451 --> 00:20:04.205
to understand what's gonna
happen to our coastal cities,

00:20:04.205 --> 00:20:06.062
but also for places around the world,

00:20:06.062 --> 00:20:08.602
because so much of the human
population around the world

00:20:08.602 --> 00:20:11.102
lives very close to sea level.

00:20:13.764 --> 00:20:16.132
Public policy decisions
will have to be made

00:20:16.132 --> 00:20:18.842
to shore up all that infrastructure.

00:20:18.842 --> 00:20:21.423
And those public policy
decisions need to be informed

00:20:21.423 --> 00:20:24.756
by facts, not by politics, but by facts.

00:20:31.087 --> 00:20:34.521
- The only thing we can do
now is to become the hope

00:20:34.521 --> 00:20:37.104
for the world in which we live.

00:20:38.084 --> 00:20:40.431
You personally, can become the hope

00:20:40.431 --> 00:20:42.514
in the world you live in.

00:20:43.391 --> 00:20:47.572
So, my prayer is that
one of you will awaken

00:20:47.572 --> 00:20:50.452
in this spirit and
become the guiding light

00:20:50.452 --> 00:20:52.774
for the people in the world.

00:20:52.774 --> 00:20:55.941
The world in which we live needs hope.

00:20:58.026 --> 00:21:00.109
That's the greatest need.

00:21:03.807 --> 00:21:06.390
(somber music)

